<div style="border: 2px solid #1a5276; padding: 15px; text-align: center; background-color: #e0f2f7;">
    <h1 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # 2 – Lesson # 2</h1>
    <p style="color: #1a5276; font-style: italic; margin: 5px 0 0 0;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 2</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Reading</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 12th Grade (CEFR: B1.3)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Supporting Our Community</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> Volunteering in Our Community</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Reading: Analyze articles on societal trends. Reading: Construct critical analyses of non-fiction works.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Reading: Can analyze articles on how government support impacts community services (e.g., "In the article, it says that local programs are funded by the government to improve public infrastructure. This shows that public services are influenced by government priorities.") Can construct critical analyses of non-fiction works on volunteerism (e.g., "The article discusses how the Jóvenes Unidos program helps at-risk youth and suggests that increasing partnerships with local organizations could extend its impact.") Can ask and answer questions on public service articles to explore civic responsibility (e.g., "What have volunteers been doing to improve education?" "Volunteers have been organizing programs and distributing materials to students.")</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #e0f2f7; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #e0f2f7; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #e0f2f7; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays images of local Panamanian volunteer projects (e.g., community clean-ups, educational support, food distribution at a shelter). Students are asked to briefly brainstorm in pairs what they see and identify how these activities relate to "Supporting Our Community." Teacher guides a brief whole-class discussion, eliciting key vocabulary from Lesson 1 like 'volunteers,' 'community,' 'projects,' 'services,' 'support.' Students share one idea per pair.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models how to read an opinion piece on civic responsibility, emphasizing the pronunciation of content words (e.g., 'community,' 'programs,' 'government,' 'impact') and alternating stressed and unstressed syllables for rhythm. For example, "Com-MU-ni-ty PRO-grams are FUN-ded by the GOV-ern-ment." The teacher demonstrates scanning for main ideas and identifying the author's stance before students begin reading the new text.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding of the warm-up and the concept of "civic responsibility" and "government support."</p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What does 'civic responsibility' mean in our community?" (Expected answer: "It means taking responsibility for improving our community.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Can local programs be funded by the government or only by individuals?" (Expected answer: "They can be funded by the government.")</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5-7 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Supporting Our Community."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> An authentic article from a local newspaper or online platform titled: "Government Support and Citizen Involvement: Driving Community Development in Panama." The article is approximately 300-400 words, featuring formal register, and discusses specific examples of public services, volunteer brigades, and how initiatives are funded. It integrates the target grammar (passive voice, modals for necessity, present perfect continuous) and vocabulary (e.g., 'government,' 'programs,' 'resources,' 'services,' 'responsibilities,' 'volunteers,' 'projects,' 'community,' 'influence,' 'decision-making,' 'public services,' 'development,' 'impact,' 'underserved,' 'meaningful,' 'positive,' 'crucial,' 'responsible,' 'diverse,' 'active,' 'impactful,' 'essential,' 'appropriate,' 'local,' 'supportive,' 'governmental,' 'public,' 'community-based,' 'involved,' 'collaborative,' 'high-risk').</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> The student can analyze articles on societal trends and construct critical analyses of non-fiction works.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks (Reading):</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Reading:</b> Students read the article quickly to identify the main topic. Teacher asks: "What is the main idea of this article?" and "Who is mainly responsible for community development, according to the article?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False Statements:</b> Students read the article again to determine if a series of statements are true or false (e.g., "Community programs are solely funded by individual donations." "Volunteers have been working only in urban areas."). Students must correct false statements.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10-12 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Reading.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Reading: Analyzing how government support impacts community services; identifying grammatical structures (passive voice, modals for necessity, present perfect continuous); recognizing key vocabulary related to civic responsibility and community involvement.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 1 (Controlled Practice - Grammar & Vocabulary Scan):</b> In pairs, students re-read the article, highlighting examples of:
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Passive voice sentences (e.g., "Community programs are funded by the government.").</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Modals of necessity (e.g., "Individuals should contribute to community projects.").</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Present perfect continuous (e.g., "Volunteers have been working to improve community services.").</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Five new vocabulary words from the provided list (e.g., 'influence,' 'impact,' 'underserved,' 'initiative,' 'allocate').</li>
                        </ul>
                        They then discuss what each highlighted sentence means in the context of the article.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 2 (Semi-controlled - Sentence Completion & Analysis):</b> The teacher provides sentence stems or cloze sentences directly from or inspired by the article, focusing on critical points or arguments. Students complete them using their own words based on the text. For example: "The article suggests that government support _________ (verb related to 'influence' or 'impact') local initiatives by _________ (reason/method mentioned)." Or "Volunteer brigades have been _________ (present perfect continuous verb) to address the needs of _________ (adjective like 'underserved') communities." They then justify their choices by referring to specific parts of the text.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 3 (Pair Interaction - Critical Reading Questions):</b> Students work in pairs to answer specific analysis questions about the text:
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How does the article illustrate that public services are influenced by government priorities?"</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"According to the text, what have volunteers been doing to improve education, and why is community involvement crucial for everyone?"</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Identify two responsibilities of citizens highlighted in the article regarding community development."</li>
                        </ul>
                        They share their answers, explicitly citing evidence from the text. This also practices engaging in discussions about civic responsibilities.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">15-18 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Supporting Our Community."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Collaborative Critical Analysis (Written Product / Presentation Outline)</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b> In small groups (3-4 students), students will construct a critical analysis of the article. Each group must address the following points, citing evidence from the text:</p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Summarize how government support (or lack thereof) *impacts* community services discussed in the article, using passive voice structures where appropriate (e.g., "Several initiatives *are funded* directly by the local government, which allows them to expand their reach.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Analyze what *responsibilities* individuals *should* take on, based on the article's message, integrating modals of necessity.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Describe specific actions that *volunteers have been undertaking* to improve different areas of the community, using the present perfect continuous tense.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Propose one *diverse solution* or extension to a volunteer *initiative* mentioned in the article, explaining how it could further *develop* community *involvement* and *address underserved* populations. (This integrates sociolinguistic and pragmatic functions).</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">The groups will produce either a short written analysis (100-120 words) or an outline for a presentation, which they will keep for the next lesson (Speaking). Emphasis is on clear expression and accurate use of target grammar and vocabulary found in the article.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10-12 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Groups submit their written critical analysis or presentation outline from Stage 4.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher Observation/Rubric):</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity of analysis and summarization of article points.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Appropriate use of target vocabulary from the lesson and article (e.g., 'impact,' 'influence,' 'resources,' 'underserved,' 'initiative').</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Accurate integration of passive voice, modals for necessity, and present perfect continuous.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Evidence of critical thinking in proposing solutions or explaining societal trends.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Collaboration within the group during the task.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete a quick exit ticket individually.</p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"One new idea about government's role in community development that I learned from the article is..."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I found it easiest/most challenging to (analyze the impact of government, identify grammar, propose solutions, understand specific vocabulary) because..."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part of the article made the biggest 'impact' on your understanding of civic responsibility?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How has this reading changed your 'perspective' on the 'involvement' of 'volunteers' in local 'projects'?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> What reading comprehension strategies worked best for students to analyze the article? Were students able to effectively identify and use the target grammar (passive voice, modals, present perfect continuous) in their analysis? What adjustments or additional scaffolds might be needed for students to further construct critical analyses of non-fiction works, especially regarding vocabulary development and complex sentence structures? Did the pronunciation focus (stressed/unstressed syllables) aid their understanding or reading aloud? How well did the groups lead collaborative discussions?</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> The critical analyses and presentation outlines developed in this reading lesson will serve as the basis for the next lesson (Lesson 3: Speaking & Interaction), where students will orally present and discuss their findings and proposed solutions to their peers, engaging in collaborative discussions about volunteer projects and offering diverse solutions.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">3 min</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>